From: Brady <brady@aioscilloscope.com>
Subject: gpiolib, regmap: two informational notices at info level
Copyright (C) 2026 Olympian LED, Inc.

Two notices are printed at info level instead of warning level: gpiolib's
note that a chip uses a static GPIO base (the Rockchip numbering is relied
on by user space), and regmap's note that a device without cache defaults is
read back from the hardware once.

---
diff --git a/drivers/base/regmap/regcache.c b/drivers/base/regmap/regcache.c
--- a/drivers/base/regmap/regcache.c
+++ b/drivers/base/regmap/regcache.c
@@ -52,7 +52,8 @@ static int regcache_hw_init(struct regmap *map)
 
 	if (!map->reg_defaults_raw) {
 		bool cache_bypass = map->cache_bypass;
-		dev_warn(map->dev, "No cache defaults, reading back from HW\n");
+		/* The documented fallback for a device with no default table (e.g. PMICs). */
+		dev_info(map->dev, "No cache defaults, reading back from HW\n");
 
 		/* Bypass the cache access till data read from HW */
 		map->cache_bypass = true;
diff --git a/drivers/gpio/gpiolib.c b/drivers/gpio/gpiolib.c
--- a/drivers/gpio/gpiolib.c
+++ b/drivers/gpio/gpiolib.c
@@ -1013,7 +1013,12 @@ int gpiochip_add_data_with_key(struct gpio_chip *gc, void *data,
 			 */
 			gc->base = base;
 		} else {
-			dev_warn(&gdev->dev,
+			/*
+			 * Information, not a fault: a board whose GPIO numbers are a
+			 * user-space ABI (the Rockchip bank*32 bases with GPIO_SYSFS) sets
+			 * them on purpose.
+			 */
+			dev_info(&gdev->dev,
 				 "Static allocation of GPIO base is deprecated, use dynamic allocation.\n");
 		}
 
